From c9fbed77f16ff96766d33359c89064fb83ad97e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 20:19:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Fix FNV lookup table use-after-free; resolve lookup.csv
 at runtime

DllMain called ReadLookupTable on DLL_THREAD_ATTACH and ReleaseLookupTable
on DLL_THREAD_DETACH. Those fire for every thread the host process creates or
destroys, so any thread exiting anywhere in a Unity editor deleted the global
FNV table while other threads were reading it. A concurrent level load -
several workers reading STR chunks, each calling FNV::Lookup - then
dereferenced freed memory. It crashed inside Hashing.cpp, which resolves to
CalcLowerCRC under deferred symbols and looks unrelated to threading.

The table is now a function-local static: exactly-once thread-safe init by the
C++11 standard, never torn down, so Lookup is a pure read needing no lock.
DllMain does nothing. That also removes file I/O from under the loader lock,
where it could deadlock against another module loading on a second thread.

Separately, LOOKUP_CSV_PATH is an absolute path into the build machine's source
tree. Ship the DLL elsewhere and the ifstream silently opens nothing, leaving
every hash-named string unresolved with no diagnostic. The path is now resolved
relative to the loaded module, falling back to the compile-time path so a
developer's build tree keeps working, and warns when neither resolves.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
